                                     wuimg
                              Frugal image viewer

wu is a minimalistic but not barebones image viewer. It aims for comfort,
speed, accurate color rendering, and format documentation/preservation.

wu is meant as a terminal companion, so launching from one is recommended.


Features
--------
* Few keybindings
* No graphical elements besides the image; all status text goes to stdout
* Displays EXIF, XMP, and format-specific metadata
* Support for embedded ICC profiles and format-specific colorspaces
  * At the moment, images are converted to sRGB for display
* Can browse archives, via libarchive
* Doesn't modify the original file when rotating!
* Runs on Wayland, DRM/KMS, and X11


Supported formats
-----------------
Over 40 format families are supported, ranging from the common to the niche.
Here is a representative but not exhaustive listing:
* Well-known:
  * BMP and ICO
  * GIF
  * JPEG
  * PNG
  * SVG
  * TGA
  * TIFF
* 21st century:
  * AVIF and HEIF
  * JPEG-XL
  * WEBP
* Legacy:
  * ILBM
  * MAKI and MAG
  * MSX-BASIC
  * PCX
  * Yanagisawa's Pi and PIC
* Specialty:
  * RAW camera formats
  * Sixel
* Uncompressed:
  * DPX
  * Farbfeld
  * The whole PNM extended family (including PAM, Pfm, Xv thumbnail, and MTV)
* Video-games/Visual novels:
  * RealLive's G00 and PDT
  * PSX's TIM

See the files under 'dec/' or run 'meson configure' for a full list. Files
under 'lib/' are our home-made decoders, which usually document the format
in some way.

As archives are supported, CBZ/CB7 are also viewable.


Prerequisites
-------------
For building you'll need
* meson
* A C and C++ compiler (both GCC and Clang work fine, others... probably not)

and at a very minimum the development files for
* libarchive
* libepoxy
* libexiv2
* libicu
* liblcms2
* libuchardet

Window backends and image decoders can be enabled or disabled independently.
The default is to compile them if the required dev files are detected, so
usually no further configuration is needed beyond having them installed.

For window backends you need:
* Wayland
  * libwayland
  * libxkbcommon
  * wayland-protocols
* DRM/KMS
  * libdrm
  * libgbm
* X11
  * libglfw3

For image decoding:
* libavif
* libflif
* libgif
* libheif
* libjbig
* libjpeg
* libopenjp2 (JPEG2000)
* libcharls (JPEG-LS)
* libjxl
* liblerc
* libpng
* libraw
* librsvg
* libtiff
* libwebp and libwebpdemux

To actually run the program you'll need
* Linux (it might work on other POSIX systems, I don't know)
* OpenGL 3.3
* 128MB of RAM minimum, 256MB recommended


Compiling
---------
Pick a good name for a build directory and run

	meson BUILDDIR

This will by default enable as many formats as possible. In case you want to
explicitely disable or enable some (like for, say, fast fuzzing because you
don't trust me or the C language in general), run something like

	meson configure -D png=disabled -D maki=enabled BUILDDIR

You may also add `--auto-features disable` to disable everything by default.

Once you're ready, just run

	meson install -C BUILDDIR

A lone executable `wu` will be produced, and copied to whatever location meson
uses as default. This might require root privileges though. To set a different
location, do

	meson configure --prefix PREFIX BUILDDIR

beforehand so that it's installed to $PREFIX/bin/ from then on. A good
candidate is '~/.local' if that's on your $PATH.

That's it. Run 'wu -h' to get some help text. The help screen is short (it
may actually fit into your screen) and contains all the information you need.


Usage
-----
Three keys you need to remember: 'q' to quit, 'n' to go to the next image, and
'p' for the previous one.

Commands:
	`wu [FILE|DIR]` for viewing.
	`wu -h` for command-line usage
	`wu -k` for a list of keybindings


Configuration
-------------
All done through a 'key = value #comment' file that must be located at
"$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wu.conf" or "$HOME/.config/wu.conf". 'key' has to be a member
of the wu_conf struct, which is defined at 'conf.h' with plenty of comments.
You should take a look at it.

Example:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comment                     # A comment.
magnify_under = 128           # (unsigned) int. An (unsigned) integer.
initial_size = 1280 720       # struct. Specified in order.
bg = 0xcc 0x66 0x99 0x33      # array. Specified in order.
bg_source = metadata          # enum. Specified as the value name sans prefix
no_window_decorations = true  # bool. Specified as 'true' or 'false'. Don't you
                              # try to cheat using 0 or 1.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Party tricks
------------
Have a weird file from the twentieth century, and want to use it in a modern
environment?

	wu w weird.pi  # Writes to 'weird.pi.pam'

Same, but you'd rather keep it in a more compact format?

	wu w -s weird.pi | convert - weird.png   # using imagemagick, or...
	wu w -s weird.pi | pamtopng > weird.png  # using netpbm
